  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nThunderstorms continue to the south and east of Pittsburgh\nduring these predawn hours, as elevated instability continues\nahead of an advancing shortwave trough. Low, but non-zero risk\nfor gusty to marginally severe wind will continue with these\nstorms until they exit the region by 09Z or so, as the \nshortwave departs and a surface boundary enters the region. \nAfter the storms exit, clearing and a moist ground may allow for\npatchy fog/stratus development in the hours leading up to \nsunrise, especially in locations that received heavy rainfall.\nThese would mix out by mid-morning.\n\nAfter sunrise, surface ridging and mid-level capping will\nsuppress convective development through Sunday and into the\nevening, providing a welcome break from the daily storm chances.\nThis will occur even as the front potentially stalls in our\nregion, which will likely have implications beyond today. A \ndrop in dewpoints, mainly into the mid 60s, will also provide a \nreprieve from the sticky conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nDaily shower and thunderstorms chances return for Monday and\nbeyond. Very broad troughing will remain in place until at \nleast Wednesday before a stronger shortwave deepens the trough \nover the Great Lakes. With prevailing W-NW flow and disturbances\npassing through the upper pattern, there will be daily chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms, with the potential for more \norganized convective complexes as hinted to in some of the \nguidance. \n\nThe first of these, occurring late Sunday night into Monday\nmorning, appears most likely to dive its most impactful storms \nwest of the CWA, into central/western Ohio, according to most of\nthe HREF component members. At least scattered showers and\nstorms may arrive after 06Z, but are most likely to remain non-\nsevere given the time of day/likely lack of sufficient\ninstability.\n\nExactly what happens later on Monday remains murky, and will\nlargely depend on the environment/boundaries left behind by the\nmorning complex, as well as potential reinvigoration of its\nremnants. Also, the position of the front that stalls in our \nregion today may play a role in determining where the best \nchances for additional development may occur. The environment\nwill certainly be sufficient to support severe storms, with\n1500-2500 J/kg of MLCAPE possible, and with around 30 knots of \n0-6km shear supporting storm organization. Damaging wind gusts \nremain the primary threat, but hail and tornado risks also are \npresent. Also of note: given the continued presence of above- \nnormal precipitable water values in a climatologic sense, as \nwell as the potential for some training storms along the \nboundary, WPC continued to highlight our region in a Slight \n(level 2 of 4) Risk for excessive rain for Monday into Monday \nnight. The eventual need for a Flood Watch cannot be ruled out.\n\nSevere/heavy rain risks continue for Tuesday and potentially\ninto Wednesday as well, as the boundary may continue to waver \naround the area in a moist, unstable air mass. Machine-learning \nguidance is in support of this. The stronger Great Lakes\nshortwave may finally push the boundary definitively south of\nthe region by Thursday/Friday, leading to less threatening\nweather.",
